23:09:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand the confusion—it feels counterintuitive. After all, the movie was already made, the discs were pressed, and people have bought them. So why can’t the studio just… keep selling that same version? The key is understanding that music rights are nearly always **leased for a specific window of time**, not bought permanently. Once that lease expires, the studio loses the legal right to **manufacture new copies or distribute the film in any way**, even if it’s the exact same edit that was previously released. Let’s break it down step by step, because this is where the mental block usually happens. ### 1. The License Covers Distribution, Not Just Creation The legal permission to use a song has two parts: - **Synchronization License (“sync license”):** The right to pair the music with the moving images. This is usually a one-time permission from the publisher. - **Master Use License:** The right to use the specific sound recording (the version you hear on the radio). This is from the record label. Both of these are contracts that spell out exactly **how, where, and for how long** the film can be shown. The most important parameter is often the **Term**—a start date and an end date. A typical agreement might say: “You may sell this film on VHS and DVD in North America for 10 years from the date of first release.” When that date arrives, the permission evaporates. The studio doesn’t just lose the right to make *new* deals; they lose the right to **continue distributing the film**. Every new DVD or Blu‑ray that rolls off the production line after the expiry date is a copyright violation. Every digital copy sold on iTunes or streamed on Netflix is a fresh act of distribution that requires a valid license. ### 2. Think of It Like a Limited‑Time Bakery Lease Imagine you’re a baker. You rent a famous secret recipe from a chef to make a special cake for 5 years. You build a whole bakery around it, print menus, and sell thousands of cakes. After 5 years, the rental agreement ends. The chef says, “You can’t make my cake anymore.” You might think, *“But I already have the recipe, the ovens, and a line of customers!”* Legally, you can’t. You can’t bake one single new cake using that recipe, because your permission to use it has expired. The fact that you made and sold it for five years doesn’t give you the right to keep doing it forever. The movie studio is in exactly the same position with a song—they rented the right to “bake it into” new copies for a fixed period. **What about the cakes (discs) you already sold?** Those are fine. The people who bought them own them forever. But you, the baker/studio, are forbidden from making and selling any more. ### 3. What Happens When the License Expires? This is why music gets replaced for *future* sales and new formats, not necessarily the copies already on your shelf. - **Physical Media (DVD/Blu‑ray):** As soon as the license expires, the studio must stop pressing new discs. If they still have a warehouse full of unsold discs made during the license period, they might be allowed to sell those off (it depends on the contract), but they cannot manufacture another batch. When that stock runs out, the film either goes out of print or a new version is prepared with replacement music. That’s why you often see “Newly re‑edited soundtrack” on a re‑release. - **Streaming and Digital Sales:** This is where it gets even trickier. Every time a movie is streamed, the platform is technically making a copy (buffering) and publicly performing it. This is a continuous, ongoing distribution. The license must be valid at the moment of transmission. If the 10‑year term ends, the film must be taken down immediately—or a new, music‑swapped version must be uploaded that doesn’t require that expired license. - **Television Broadcasts:** Same as streaming. Each broadcast is a new public performance license event. An expired song can’t be aired. ### 4. The “Why Not Re‑License?” Factor You might then ask, “Well, why don’t they just pay to extend the license?” They try. But: - The artist or rights holder might ask for a sum so large (now that the movie is a known hit) that it eats up all the profit from future sales. - The rights holder might simply refuse, perhaps because they’ve signed an exclusive deal with another entity or they no longer approve of the film’s content. - The contract might be lost in a corporate merger and nobody can even confirm who holds the rights anymore, making it legally risky to continue. In those cases, the studio has two choices: **retire the film entirely** (pull it from all future sales and streaming), or **surgically remove the problematic song** and insert something they have perpetual, cheap rights to. The latter is usually the only commercially viable option. So, in short: the original version you remember *couldn’t* just keep being sold, because selling is itself the act that the expired license forbids. It’s not about the version being “the original,” it’s about the legal right to sell that version having a built‑in expiration date from the moment the contract was signed.
