23:10:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a sharp observation, and you’re absolutely right to call out the seeming contradiction. Some movies *do* sit on shelves for decades with their original music completely intact. The reason isn’t that my earlier explanation was wrong—it’s that there’s a crucial distinction between two completely different types of music in films: **the original score** and **licensed pre‑existing songs.** That distinction is the missing piece that makes everything fall into place. ### The Two Completely Different Categories of Music in a Movie When you think of a “classic film” getting a 20th‑anniversary edition with all the music you remember, 95% of the time you’re thinking of the **original film score**. Using the same song a studio commissioned in 1975 is trivial. Using the same *pop hit from the radio* they licensed in 1975 is a legal minefield. #### 1. The Original Score (Composed for the Film) This is music written specifically for the movie—think John Williams’ *Star Wars*, Ennio Morricone’s *The Good, the Bad and the Ugly*, or Bernard Herrmann’s *Psycho*. - **Who owns it?** The studio almost always owns the copyright to the score, or has a “work‑for‑hire” agreement that grants them permanent, worldwide, all‑media rights. They paid the composer to create it for them. - **Any time limits?** No. They own it outright. They can keep repressing *Jaws* DVDs for the next 100 years with the famous shark theme, and no license ever expires. That’s why a 20th‑anniversary edition of a classic orchestral masterpiece doesn’t need music replacement. The music is a permanent asset of the film, like the camera negative. #### 2. Licensed Pre‑Existing Songs (The “Needle Drops”) This is when a filmmaker includes a song that already existed—a Beatles track, a Motown hit, a pop song from the radio. - **Who owns it?** The record label and music publisher. The studio is just a renter. - **Any time limits?** Yes, almost always. The license is for a finite term, specific formats, and specific territories. This is what causes all the problems. ### So Where Did “In Perpetuity” Deals Come From? You’re remembering correctly that some older films with licensed songs *do* seem to stay in print forever without changes (e.g., the 20th‑anniversary edition of *Forrest Gump* still has all its ‘60s and ‘70s hits). This happens for two reasons: - **Some older contracts were genuinely perpetual and all‑inclusive.** Before home video and streaming were foreseeable, a far‑sighted or lucky negotiation might have included language like “all media now known or hereafter devised, in perpetuity, throughout the universe.” If the studio secured that golden clause in 1985, they can keep re‑releasing that movie forever. These deals were expensive back then, and they’re astronomically expensive now, but they *do* exist for some classic films. When you see an anniversary edition unchanged, it’s often because the original deal was rock‑solid and future‑proof. - **The film was re‑released *while the original license was still valid*.** A “20th anniversary” edition isn’t automatically a new, separate deal. If the original license was for 25 years, the studio can press a shiny new Blu‑ray box set in year 20 and it’s still covered. You see it on shelves and assume it’s an eternal item, but the clock is still ticking. Five years later, that same edition might quietly go out of print and be replaced by a version with altered music that you don’t notice because you already own the old one. ### Why the Difference Matters for Your Question Your original question was, *“Why do they change the music in movies?”* The movies that get their music changed are overwhelmingly **ones that relied on popular, pre‑existing songs** and whose licenses have simply run out. A film scored entirely by a composer (which accounts for the vast majority of films made before the 1980s and many still today) almost never faces this problem. So your counterexample is both true and perfectly consistent: - *Gone with the Wind* can be re‑released forever because its music was a commissioned score. - *Dirty Dancing* (which relies heavily on pre‑existing hits like “(I’ve Had) The Time of My Life”) caused licensing headaches for later re‑releases because those songs are owned by outside labels, and some of the original deals had finite terms. (Actually, for *Dirty Dancing*, they famously managed to secure perpetual rights for the signature song because it was recorded specifically for the film, but many other films with off‑the‑shelf pop hits weren’t so lucky.) **In short:** The fact that some classic films on store shelves still have their original music doesn’t contradict the time‑limited license explanation. It just reveals that those specific films either: 1. Used a fully studio‑owned score, or 2. Had the exceptional foresight (and budget) to negotiate perpetual licenses for their needle‑drop songs, or 3. Were still within their original license window when you saw them, and may have changed later. The frustrating, music‑swapped re‑releases you’ve heard about are the casualties of a system where most licensed songs are rentals, not purchases. And because popular songs are what often date a movie and make it nostalgic, their removal is jarring and noticeable—exactly why we talk about it.
