23:11:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why that seems like the obvious, logical way to do it. You buy a costume, a prop, a camera, a script—why not just buy the song? Here's the blunt reality: **They almost never "buy" a pre-existing song because the seller almost never wants to sell it, and the price would be astronomical if they did.** When you hear a famous pop song in a movie, the studio isn't buying a physical object. They're renting a piece of intellectual property that the original creators and record labels treat as a forever money-making asset. Selling it outright is like selling the goose that lays golden eggs. Let's break down why the "just buy it" model doesn't work for music. ### 1. It's Not One Item, It's Two Separate Intellectual Properties A recorded song is actually two distinct copyrights with two different owners: - **The Composition (Publishing Rights):** The words and melody, owned by the songwriter and a music publisher. Think of this as the sheet music. - **The Sound Recording (Master Rights):** That specific recorded version you know from the radio, owned by the record label. To "own" a song outright, the movie studio would have to negotiate a permanent buyout with *both* the publisher and the label. That means convincing two completely separate corporate entities to give up a revenue-generating asset forever. For a hit song that might be earning millions a year in radio play, streaming, commercials, and covers, a one-time buyout price would be so large it could exceed the entire music budget of the film—and even then, they might simply say no because they don't want to lose long-term control. ### 2. Artists and Labels Don't Want to Lose Control Remember, the record label and the artist deeply care about how their song is used. They don't want to sell it to a studio that could then: - Re-use it in a cheap straight-to-DVD sequel without asking. - License it out to a third party for a tasteless ad campaign, forever associating their song with something they hate. - Chop it up or remix it in a way that damages the song's reputation. A time-limited, specific-use license gives them control. An outright sale hands that control over completely. Most major artists (or their estates) will not agree to that. ### 3. The Budget Reality: Movies Are Made on Rented Music The entire economics of filmmaking are built on licensing, not buying, popular music. A standard sync + master license for a well-known song might cost anywhere from $10,000 to $100,000+ for a few years of limited use. A perpetual, all-encompassing buyout of that same song? You're effectively asking to buy a piece of music history. The price would likely land in the millions, and many rights holders would still refuse at any price. ### 4. So, Do They Ever "Own" a Song in a Film? Yes—When They Make It Themselves. This is the critical exception that proves the rule. The one time a studio *does* own a song outright is when they commission it to be written and recorded specifically for the film—a "work for hire." In that case, the studio is the publisher and the owner of the master from day one. That's why: - The theme from *Titanic* ("My Heart Will Go On") was recorded by Celine Dion but the copyright and master are controlled by Sony (the film's music arm). They effectively own that recording in the context of the film. - The *Dirty Dancing* song "(I've Had) The Time of My Life" was written and recorded for the film, so RCA (the label) and the publishers likely gave the studio a special perpetual license baked into the production deal. That's why you never hear about that song being replaced. - The entire soundtrack to *Saturday Night Fever* was mostly existing Bee Gees hits, but the Bee Gees were heavily involved in the film, and the album was a cultural phenomenon. Deals were structured in a unique, collaborative way that essentially tied the music to the film permanently. But for the vast majority of needle-drop moments—like using "Sweet Home Alabama" in a road trip movie—the song existed long before the script was written, and its owners have no reason to sell their golden goose. They just rent it for a while. So, you're right that it feels natural to "just buy the song," but in the music industry, songs aren't sold like props. They're leased like highly protected, revenue-churning assets. That's why, decades later, when the lease ends, the song has to be pulled out of the film—because they never owned it in the first place.
