s Oscar‑targeting event, so the math worked. 2. **The artists and labels often won’t sell perpetual rights at any price.** Even if a studio offers a fortune, a major artist’s estate or a label like Sony Music might simply refuse to hand over permanent control of a beloved recording. In *Forrest Gump*’s case, the producers got buy‑in because the project was so prestigious that many rights holders saw it as an honor and a guaranteed album‑sales booster. So, your DVD of *Forrest Gump* *is* the same as it ever was, and will remain so forever, precisely because the producers refused to play the “time‑limited rental” game that plagues almost every other movie with a pop soundtrack. It’s the glowing exception that proves how the system usually works—and it cost a fortune and a minor miracle in contract negotiations to make it happen.
