23:13:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get it. You’re looking at a physical pile of evidence on your shelf. Every disc has the songs you remember. Nobody came to your house and swapped them. From your perspective, music replacement sounds like a myth. Your experience is completely valid—and it’s also exactly what you’d expect, even in a world where music licenses expire all the time. Here’s why your shelf full of unchanged DVDs is perfectly consistent with everything I’ve described. ### First: The Discs You Own Are Frozen in Time The DVD you bought in 2004 is a physical artifact manufactured during a specific license window. If the license was valid when that disc was pressed, that disc is legal forever. No one can ever force you to stop owning it or change the songs inside it. You’re holding a snapshot from a period when the studio had permission. What you’re not seeing on your shelf is what happened *after* that pressing ran out. When the license expired, the studio simply stopped making that version. Any new production run either used different music or didn’t happen at all. The old discs gradually disappeared from store shelves as stock sold through, but you already had yours. So your collection never changed. You’re seeing the before-picture, not the after. ### Second: Where the Changes Actually Show Up (And Why You May Have Missed Them) Music replacement typically doesn’t happen to the disc you already own. It happens in these places, which you might not encounter if you’re mostly a physical media re-watcher: - **A later DVD or Blu‑ray reissue:** That “20th Anniversary Special Edition” might have a revised soundtrack, but you never bought it because you already own the original. You wouldn’t know unless you compared them. - **Streaming versions:** The copy on Netflix or Amazon Prime might be a newer master with replaced music, while your DVD on the shelf remains pristine. If you never stream the movie, you’d never see the change. - **TV broadcasts:** Syndicated version